# Closing keywords are executable — always apply

Before publishing any text GitHub will render for a pull request — the body, the title, a commit
message — check it for `close`, `closes`, `closed`, `fix`, `fixes`, `fixed`, `resolve`, `resolves`
or `resolved` followed by a reference to an issue or pull request. When that text reaches the
default branch, **GitHub closes whatever the reference points at**, in any repository the merging
account can write to, and a pull request is as valid a target as an issue.

Measured against GitHub itself on 2026-09-09, fifteen shapes, not recalled from documentation:

- **Every reference form fires**: `#N`, `owner/repo#N`, a bare URL, an angle-bracket autolink
  `<url>`, and a markdown link `[any text](url)`.
- **Meaning does not matter; adjacency does.** `This does not fix #7` closes #7. A colon after the
  keyword is fine. What stops it is a token between the keyword and the reference: a word, a
  parenthesis, a newline.
- **Inert forms**: a code span (`` `owner/repo#N` ``), an HTML comment, or a word between
  (`the fix is in`, `opened as`, `fixed in`).
- **Three surfaces**: the body, the title, and the commit message. A squash merge copies the body
  into the commit.
- **No pre-merge signal exists for a pull-request target**: GitHub's `willCloseTarget` is `false` for
  them, so nothing on GitHub's side will warn you.

The dangerous shape is a write-up about an open piece of work that merges before the work does — an
investigation body linking its fix, a lesson linking the change it came from, a sibling-repo pull
request linking the other half. In one month it closed three pull requests at the organisation
that wrote this rule: one attributed to the human who merged the write-up, one the author's own
fix closed for five hours by his own investigation, and one closed from an automated agent's
lesson body and then announced in chat as a rejection.

## Do this

Say it without the keyword touching the link: `Opened as <link>`, `The change is <link>`,
`fixed in <link>`. Or wrap the reference in a code span. Before pushing, run the check over what
you wrote:

```bash
grep -nEi '\b(close[sd]?|fix(e[sd])?|resolve[sd]?)\b:?[[:space:]]*(\[[^]]*\]\(https?://github\.com/[^)]+/(pull|issues)/[0-9]+|<?https?://github\.com/[^/[:space:]]+/[^/[:space:]]+/(pull|issues)/[0-9]+|([A-Za-z0-9_.-]+/[A-Za-z0-9_.-]+)?#[0-9]+)' <file>
```

A hit is a line GitHub will act on. An automated agent that opens pull requests should run the
equivalent parser over every title, body and commit it writes. An agent opening a pull request by
hand gets no such check, which is what this rule is for.

Use the keyword on purpose only when the target **should** close on merge and it is in the same
repository you are merging into — the case GitHub designed it for.

## Forbidden

- `fix`, `close` or `resolve` in any tense directly before `#N`, `owner/repo#N`, a GitHub URL, an
  autolink or a markdown link, in a pull request body, title or commit message, unless closing that
  target on merge is the intent.
- Relying on negation, tense or surrounding prose to make the keyword inert. Only a token between,
  a code span or an HTML comment does.
- Reading a pull request that closed with no review and no comment as somebody's decision. Read the
  timeline's `closed` event actor first; a close in the same second as a merge elsewhere by that
  actor is this mechanism, and a one-call `PATCH` with `state=open` restores it untouched.

## Why this exists

Added 2026-09-09, after the fifteen-shape probe above and the three closed pull requests it
explained.
